a phrase we've all heard at some point  in the industry consider the player dumb  you have to make things crystal clear  for them blue tarps yellow tags and so  on this quote comes from julian eva a  level designer from arcane studios to  his credit julian went on to say i think  that's criminally wrong i'd be curious  to know if that thinking is as present  in the japanese industry he also makes  it clear these are his views alone and  shouldn't be linked to any particular  studio and he's also not saying every  studio falls into that trap julian  recently had a very interesting twitter  thread where he took a look at elden  ring and gave some of his observations  coming from a game developer i think  you'll find many of these pretty  interesting for anyone who's played  elden ring you'll know there's something  from software has figured out something  they've mastered like few other devs in  the industry today and it all starts  with trust julian notes that elden ring  players are deeply trusted and he feels  it's been a long time since western  culture took a step back from such basic  values especially in open worlds as of  may 11 2022 elden ring had sold more  than 13 million copies since its launch  earlier this year which makes it far and  away the best-selling title from  software has ever released with these  staggering numbers it leaves players  like me to wonder if this could be the  beginning of a shift in how western  developers look at some of their design  choices in games julian says he feels  like eldon ring assumes the player  adapts and craves to experiment ideas  thus embracing the fact of shaking off  the expectations from time to time by  playing a lot with affordance to  possibly ridiculous extents he shared  this screenshot along with that comment  you can see here that it shows a section  of the game with an area that appears to  lead somewhere but the possible pathway  is collapsed it's not a jump you can  make on foot and yet it looks like it  should be well within the range of your  loyal steed torrent but a horse scaling  the side of a tower this is precisely  the affordance to ridiculous extents  he's talking about and for me speaking  from a player's side of things that is  what makes elden ring's world shine not  only is it a vast open world to explore  it's filled with depth and layers that  aren't clear at the beginning of the  game or even much later in the game in  some cases there's an almost constant  feeling of wonder and curiosity any out  of place stone or small ledge could be a  hint at something to be discovered item  descriptions range from mundane and  seemingly insignificant to containing  crucial pieces of information and yet  this has to be an incredibly difficult  balance for developers to strike if the  secrets and answers are too difficult  players may grow frustrated and give up  and if they're too simple then  overcoming the challenge may not feel  rewarding to the player in elden ring  the world isn't served on a silver plate  julian writes no one is going to come to  you asking to do this or that you have  to commit to set your own goals to take  the initiative to make attempts to fail  for me it's overcoming these challenges  that often gives my greatest sense of  accomplishment in elden ring even if you  don't come away from a particularly  difficult area with a new favorite  weapon or even a huge amount of runes  you still come away feeling accomplished  and that the exploration was worth your  time he says this dynamic definitely  puts you in charge a sense of duty you  know you came that far by your own means  and all thanks to your own observation  not because you followed a bright marker  that told you to pass there or kill that  thing or press that button you are  trusted and yet this trust would mean  nothing if frum wasn't also supremely  confident in their ability to design  worlds and systems that are both  inherently intuitive and challenging at  the same time while some could certainly  mistake eldon ring's challenges or  secrets as intentionally obtuse julian  believes the biggest secrets are made as  such to encourage discussions and  provide opportunities for collaboration  with players through other means such as  social media or even the game's  multiplayer from software's trust in the  player and confidence in their ability  to execute also extends to the elden  ring multiplayer sure some players use  it as an opportunity to troll or mislead  but by and large you'll find elden ring  riddled with helpful comments and even  players dedicated to helping others  overcome some of the game's most  difficult encounters julian writes feels  like we've been paralyzed by the fear of  letting our players get frustrated or  lost for a minute or two and find the  solution by themselves we might have  been trapped at our own game making  things obvious and avoid any kind of  struggling from our players at all costs  and sure eldon ring will let you try  time and time again on the same boss  over and over even if you're way under  leveled even if you're missing a key  item that can make the fight much easier  and yes even if you're one of those  lunatics like me who refuses to summon  help but all the while these systems to  alleviate and overcome those pain points  are built right into the game sure you  can beat your head against the wall  until you get good or you can lean into  those other systems by learning how  different weapon types work and how some  are more effective against certain  bosses than others you can grind away in  the open world and upgrade your  character until they're overpowered or  even outright summon other players to  actively help you with the challenge and  that's just beginning to scratch the  surface of the options you have at your  disposal of course in a vast open world  the map is also of the utmost importance  julian had positive things to say about  that as well he notes that it's a great  tool to use for navigation and treasure  hunting nothing is told you've got to do  a tiny bit of interpretation on topology  then reach that thing you noticed again  you're trusted no one forced your eyes  on it in his opinion verbally and  textually hand-holding the player at all  times is diminishing what they're  actually capable of which includes  things like understanding by observation  intuitive experiments and attempts on  their own and perhaps most importantly  acceptance of possible failure along the  road to be clear this isn't to say that  all games should be designed in such a  way in fact quite the opposite julian  says players know what to expect from  playing games like eldon ring they know  what they signed up for that's why it's  hard to attempt this in the west we  don't foster the same things there's  definitely a place for games that  provide more guidance and a more relaxed  stress-free experience but perhaps the  need and desire for more games like  eldon ring is greater than many western  developers have previously believed but  even if that were true it's not an easy  path for developers after all elden ring  builds on the years of work from has  already done longtime fans of the dev  can see numerous elements from their  previous games that all come together to  create what i believe is truly a  masterpiece in elden ring as always  thank you all so much for watching i'll  talk to you again soon
 

